; 03: an array helper works for one element type only, and the standard
; library has none.
;
; Reading a grid of unknown size into an array needs an array that grows:
; store at n, doubling when full, then trim to the length read. Both
; life/life.l0 and maze/maze.l0 carry the same three functions for it
; (push_cell, copy_prefix, trim, 17 lines), because L0 has no module system
; and the library (l0 doc) has no array functions at all. Worse, they can
; only ever be written for one element type: without type parameters,
; push_cell over (array int) cannot store a (list int), so a program with
; arrays of two element types writes them twice. The builtins are the only
; generic array operations, the way cons and head are for lists, and the
; prelude's list helpers already show the pattern: rev_strs_onto exists
; beside rev_onto only because (list (list int)) is another type.
;
; The checker, on the last function below:
;
;   03-no-generic-array-helpers.l0:37:3: error: argument 1 of push_cell must
;   be (array int), but this is (array (list int))
;
; What it costs: 17 lines per element type per program, and the growth
; policy re-decided each time. At run time the doubling is fine: reading
; the 1,000 by 1,000 life board takes 0.02 s with 3M sets in place and no
; copies, and the 2,001 by 2,001 maze 0.09 s.

(def push_cell ((cells (array int)) (n int) (x int)) (array int)
  (if (lt n (array_len cells))
      (array_set cells n x)
      (array_set (copy_prefix cells (array_new (mul 2 (array_len cells)) 0) 0 n) n x)))

(def copy_prefix ((src (array int)) (dst (array int)) (i int) (n int)) (array int)
  (if (ge i n)
      dst
      (copy_prefix src (array_set dst i (array_get src i)) (add i 1) n)))

; The same growth, needed for the lines of a file: does not check.
(def push_line ((lines (array (list int))) (n int) (line (list int))) (array (list int))
  (push_cell lines n line))
